# Line endings, pinned.
#
# Without this, Git on Windows (where `core.autocrlf=true` is the installer's
# default) rewrites every text file to CRLF on checkout. A working tree that has
# LF then disagrees with what Git is about to write, so the next command reports
# the whole file as changed and every commit carries a diff that has no content
# in it. `eol=lf` overrides `core.autocrlf` and makes the repository, the
# working tree and CI (Linux) agree on one ending.
#
# `text=auto` lets Git decide what is text by looking at the content, so a file
# that is added later does not need a rule of its own.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The one place CRLF is the correct answer: a `.bat`/`.cmd` run by cmd.exe can
# misbehave with LF endings.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binaries are never line-ending-converted — normalizing their bytes is
# corruption, and it happens silently.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.tar binary
*.mp4 binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary

# Generated, and huge in a diff: keep the lockfile out of review.
package-lock.json -diff linguist-generated
